  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nNow through Tuesday Night...\n\nSurface high pressure and northwesterly upper-level flow continue to \nprevail across the region this morning. With relatively calm winds \nand clear skies, decent radiational cooling will allow for daybreak \nconditions to be rather chilly. While it will not be as cold as \nyesterday, a Frost Advisory remains in effect until 10 AM EST for \ntemperatures in the low to mid 30s. River valley fog is expected to \ndevelop over the next few hours, quickly dissipating after sunrise.\n\nThe persisting surface and upper air regimes will result in near \nnormal temperatures and dry conditions. A return to widespread highs \nin the 60s comes this afternoon, continuing into Tuesday. There is \nthe potential for some locations to reach 70 Tuesday afternoon, with \nthe highest likelihood in west central Kentucky (40-60% chance per \nlatest REFS). Overnight lows will still be on the colder side, \nranging from the mid 30s to low 40s Monday and Tuesday night. \nChances for frost will decrease after this morning, with the latest \nREFS, NBM, and LREF outputting less than 15% chances for \ntemperatures below 35. \n\nBy Tuesday afternoon, the upper-level pattern briefly shifts to more \nof a zonal regime. In addition, a weak shortwave will move eastward \nand skirt by just off to our south. Even if the wave did shift its \ntrajectory northward, the limited moisture in place would prevent \nany appreciable precipitation from occurring over us.\n\nWednesday through Sunday...\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern begins to shift considerably by the \nmiddle portions of the work week. As a shortwave over the northern \nPlains swigs southeastward, the subtropical and polar jets will \nbegin to interact and phase with each other. The combination of \nthese features will result in cyclogenesis and the formation of a \nrobust low pressure system over the eastern Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley regions. \n\nChances for precipitation will return by late Wednesday into \nThursday as the cold front from the low begins to push through the \narea. The timing, placement, and progression of the shortwave and \nthe phasing of the subtropical and polar jets will play an important \nrole as to how much precipitation we get. Another factor in this \nwill be the limited moisture return ahead of the system moving \nthorugh. While a narrow swath of PW values of 0.6-0.75\" (~75th \npercentile climatologically) will help us get some accumulations, \nensemble guidance is only outputting less than 0.5\" through Friday \nacross the area.\n\nOnce the cold front passes through, a cold airmass will fill in from \nbehind, setting up a cool end to the work week, as well as the \nweekend. Afternoon highs on Thursday will be in the 50s, dropping to \nthe mid to upper 40s Friday and Saturday. Temperatures Friday \nmorning will be near or just above freezing, with a return to \nwidespread below freezing temperatures Saturday and Sunday morning. \nIf enough post-frontal moisture sticks around the area, there is the \nchance for some snow flurries late Thursday into Friday, especially \nalong and east of the I-75 corridor. A few of the LREF members do \npick up on light snowfall, so we will continue to see how things \ntrend in the guidance as the week progresses. In addition to the \ncold air filling in behind the front, surface high pressure will \nbuild in overhead, resulting in dry conditions going into next \nweekend.",
